  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Suppose there is a number $x$ for which $2^x=3$. Show that $x$ is not a rational number. Hint: this will be a proof by contradiction.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x$ is irrational.\n\nIf $x=\\frac{p}{q}$ with positive integers $p,q$, then $2^p=3^q$, contradicting parity.",
      "worked": "Suppose $x$ is rational. Since $2^x=3>1$, $x>0$, so write $x=\\frac{p}{q}$ with positive integers $p,q$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2^{p/q}&=3\\\\(2^{p/q})^q&=3^q\\\\2^p&=3^q\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is even and the right-hand side is odd, a contradiction. Therefore $x$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
